Research Paper: The Informational Requirements of Agency
Title: Asymmetric Information and the Suspension of Will: An Analysis of Clandestine Substance Administration in Interpersonal Systems
I. Introduction
The exercise of free will is contingent upon a subject's access to an accurate representation of their environment and internal state. In the context of intimate partnerships, a "social contract of truth" is presumed to exist. When one agent intentionally alters the chemical reality of another without their knowledge—specifically through the administration of a mind-altering substance—they create a state of informational asymmetry. This paper argues that such an act constitutes a suspension of the uninformed party's agency. By replacing an individual’s informed perception with a managed simulation, the deceiving agent effectively assumes control over the other's deliberative process.
II. The Epistemic Basis of Autonomy
Autonomy is defined as the capacity for self-governance. For an action to be truly autonomous, the agent must be aware of the variables influencing their decision-making process.
 * Informational Coherence: To make a "free" choice, an individual’s internal experience must align with external reality. If an agent believes they are sober but is physiologically intoxicated, their subsequent choices are based on a false premise.
 * The Erasure of Informed Consent: Consent is not a one-time agreement but a continuous requirement for any intervention in another's biological state. The absence of disclosure is a per se invalidation of consent.
III. Epistemic Violence: The Denial of Reality
Epistemic violence, in this context, refers to the systematic undermining of a person’s capacity as a "knower."
 * Testimonial Dismissal: When the uninformed party experiences physiological changes but is told those changes are non-existent, they are forced to discount their own sensory data.
 * The Resulting Fragility: This creates a dependency where the uninformed party must rely on the deceiving agent’s version of reality because their own senses have been made "unreliable" through secret chemical intervention.
IV. The Dialectical Inquiry: For and Against
The Argument for "Paternalistic Management"
Claim: An agent may argue that the deception was a "benevolent" intervention intended to reduce the uninformed party's anxiety or friction within the relationship without causing overt harm. This view prioritizes the utility of the outcome (peace) over the integrity of the process (honesty).
The Rebuttal: The Inviolability of the Will
Counter-Claim: Even if the outcome appears positive, the process is a violation of the Categorical Imperative. By withholding the truth, the deceiving agent treats the other as a "mere means" to an end (relationship stability). One cannot claim to respect an individual’s will while simultaneously sabotaging the data that will requires to function.
V. The Ethical Obligation of the Third-Party Observer
The witness to this informational asymmetry faces a conflict of duties: the duty of confidentiality to the deceiving agent versus the duty to restore agency to the uninformed party.
 * Duty of Disclosure: Modern ethics suggests that when an individual’s bodily or mental autonomy is being secretly bypassed, the "privilege" of a secret is nullified.
 * Restorative Justice: Disclosing the truth is the only mechanism by which the uninformed party's agency can be returned to them. Truth functions as the "corrective data" that allows the system of free will to resume operation.
VI. Conclusion
A lie regarding a person’s physical or mental state is an act of ontological sabotage. While no physical harm may be intended, the metaphysical harm—the suspension of the other's free will—is absolute. The restoration of truth is not a social choice, but a requirement for the maintenance of human dignity within any interpersonal system.
